# Tool descriptions vs parameters

The tool `description` says what the tool does. It does not explain parameters.

Each parameter has its own `description`. That is where the argument is defined.

```rust
// Bad — tool text teaches `unit`
description: "Write a file. `unit` is the `.foo` that owns it."

// Good
description: "Write one source file in the project."
// unit: "Project-relative path of the `.foo` file that owns this file"
// path: "Where the file goes in the project"
```

Do not mention a parameter by name in the tool description (no `` `unit` ``, no "`path` is …").

Call it a `` `.foo` file ``. Not a bare `` `.foo` ``, not a `` `.foo` path ``.

Dest files a `.foo` file owns are **owned files**, not artifacts. A read returns `owned`.

A tool description may say what the tool returns. A parameter description says what to write. If the locator matters, say "project-relative path" on the return or on the argument — do not rename the thing.
